-- title:
--   Second inequality for solvable Galois extensions
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ and $E$ be number fields with $E$ a $k$-algebra such that $E/k$ is Galois, assume the group $E \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[k]} E$ of $k$-automorphisms of $E$ is solvable, and let $\mathfrak f$ be an ideal of $\mathcal O_k$. Work inside the group of units of the monoid of fractional ideals of $k$ and let $C_k(\mathfrak f) =$ `coprimeToModulus k 𝔣` be its subgroup of those invertible fractional ideals $I$ whose valuation $v$-count vanishes at every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_k$ dividing $\mathfrak f$. Two subgroups of $C_k(\mathfrak f)$ are formed, each by intersecting a subgroup of the ambient unit group with $C_k(\mathfrak f)$ (`Subgroup.subgroupOf`): first, `narrowRaySubgroup k 𝔣`, the subgroup generated by the invertible fractional ideals equal to $\alpha\mathcal O_k$ for some $\alpha \neq 0$ in $\mathcal O_k$ with $\alpha - 1 \in \mathfrak f$ and $\tau(\alpha) > 0$ for every ring embedding $\tau : k \to \mathbb R$; second, the image of $C_E(\mathfrak f \mathcal O_E)$ under the homomorphism sending an invertible fractional ideal $J$ of $E$ to the (finitely supported) product over the height-one primes $w$ of $\mathcal O_E$ of $\bigl(v(w)^{\,f}\bigr)^{\mathrm{count}_w(J)}$, where $v(w)$ is the prime of $\mathcal O_k$ under $w$, regarded as an invertible fractional ideal of $k$, and $f$ is `Ideal.inertiaDeg'` of $w$ over $v(w)$. The assertion is that the index in $C_k(\mathfrak f)$ of the join of these two subgroups divides the cardinality of $E \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[k]} E$.
--
--   This is the second inequality of global class field theory in its ideal-theoretic (norm-class) form, for Galois extensions of number fields with solvable Galois group; the index in question is in particular finite. It is obtained from the case of a Galois group of prime-power order together with the behaviour of the index in a tower, and it is used in the construction of the Artin symbol and the identification of its kernel with a narrow norm-ray subgroup in the Langlands–Tunnell part of the development.
